<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sinni is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, rising in the Lucanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and flowing east into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>. In antiquity it was called the Siris, and the Greek colony of Siris was founded near its mouth around 680 BCE by settlers from Colophon. Its waters are now held by the Monte Cotugno dam, which supplies Basilicata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p></div>
